-- title:
--   A uniformiser's differential spans Ω_{F/K} over a perfect field
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a perfect field and $F$ a field extension of $K$, and let $x \in F$ be an element such that $F$ is algebraic over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$, that is, a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F$ itself, and whose underlying ring is a principal ideal ring, and let $t \in F$ be an element with $\operatorname{ord}_v(t) = 1$, where $\operatorname{ord}_v(f)$ denotes the negative of the logarithm of the value of $f$ under the $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued adic valuation attached to the height-one prime of $v$. Then for every Kähler differential $\omega \in \Omega_{F/K}$ one has $$\operatorname{diffCoeff}(t,\omega) \cdot d_{K/F}t = \omega,$$ where $\operatorname{diffCoeff}(t,\omega)$ is defined to be a chosen $g \in F$ with $\omega = g \cdot dt$ if such a $g$ exists and $0$ otherwise. Equivalently: for a uniformiser $t$ at $v$, every differential of $F$ over $K$ is an $F$-multiple of $dt$, and the coefficient function returns such a multiplier.
--
--   This is the statement that an element with a simple zero at a place is a separating element, so that $dt$ generates the one-dimensional $F$-vector space $\Omega_{F/K}$, in the form needed to evaluate the coefficient function $\operatorname{diffCoeff}$; it is the perfect-constant-field analogue of the corresponding characteristic-zero result, where the hypothesis $\operatorname{ord}_v(t) \neq 0$ must be strengthened to $\operatorname{ord}_v(t) = 1$. It feeds the local theory of differentials on a curve, in particular the computation of the order of a differential at a place and the comparison of the differential divisor with the canonical divisor.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Place_diffCoeff_smul_D_of_ord_eq_one.lean

